  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1218 PM MDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nPWATs will continue a downtrend Sunday and be unsupportive of \nairmass thunderstorms across central and western NM. Isolated \nstorms are possible along a backdoor frontal boundary Sunday \nafternoon across the Northeast Highlands and East Central Plains,\nbut the 12Z model suite is largely void of qpf across our area at\nthat time. A Pacific low is forecast to wobble eastward from over\nCA on Mon/Tue, while the backdoor front is reinforced across \neastern NM. This setup will bring a resurgence in PWATs as a \nMonsoon moisture plume stands up over the region. Monday will be \nthe transition day and Tuesday is looking active. PWATs will \nchallenge calendar day records by Wednesday, which is looking \nlike the wettest day of the next seven at this time. The Pacific \nlow is forecast to fill and lift out of the picture on Wednesday, \nbut the upper high will remain centered along the upper Gulf coast\nthrough the end of the week, keeping the wet forecast going with \na Monsoon moisture plume in place over NM. Worthy of note, the \nmedium range models continue to show an active tropical pattern  \nacross the far eastern Pacific through late next week and the \nECMWF has modeled tropical remnants to move north into NM by \nFriday for two runs in a row now.",
